WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.622
Ananya, a final-year student, asks a chatbot: is

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.850
Bengaluru good for freshers? The answer appears one

3
00:00:06.885 --> 00:00:08.313
word at a time.

4
00:00:08.663 --> 00:00:11.310
That isn't a style choice. A large language

5
00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:13.725
model can only do one thing: predict the

6
00:00:13.771 --> 00:00:15.025
next token.

7
00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:18.324
First, her question is cut into tokens: chunks

8
00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:21.505
of about three or four characters. Bengaluru might

9
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.387
be three.

10
00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:25.071
You pay for every token. And every model

11
00:00:25.129 --> 00:00:27.451
has a context window: a limit on how

12
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:29.749
many tokens fit at once.

13
00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:32.653
Now the model reads every token so far,

14
00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:36.183
and gives each possible next token a probability.

15
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:38.565
It picks one, adds it to the end,

16
00:00:38.646 --> 00:00:41.131
and the longer text goes straight back in.

17
00:00:41.481 --> 00:00:45.301
Then again. And again. Repeated thousands of times,

18
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:48.633
that one trick is chat, coding and reasoning.

19
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:51.885
Temperature decides how it picks. At zero, it

20
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.904
takes the most likely token every time. Same

21
00:00:54.951 --> 00:00:56.646
question, same answer.

22
00:00:56.996 --> 00:01:00.061
Around zero point seven, less likely tokens sometimes

23
00:01:00.119 --> 00:01:02.615
win. Ask twice, and you get two different

24
00:01:02.673 --> 00:01:03.683
answers.

25
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:07.249
Now Ananya asks a follow-up: what about salaries

26
00:01:07.295 --> 00:01:11.835
there? The chatbot knows what there means. How?

27
00:01:12.185 --> 00:01:14.751
The model remembers nothing. The app sends the

28
00:01:14.797 --> 00:01:18.431
whole conversation again, inside the context window, every

29
00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:19.801
single time.

30
00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:22.845
And because it predicts plausible text, not true

31
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:25.608
text, it can say something confident and completely

32
00:01:25.654 --> 00:01:26.653
wrong.

33
00:01:27.003 --> 00:01:28.640
So here's the line that makes you sound

34
00:01:28.686 --> 00:01:32.320
like an engineer: an LLM is stateless. Memory

35
00:01:32.378 --> 00:01:33.969
is the app's job.

36
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:37.221
Next lesson: your first LLM API call in

37
00:01:37.303 --> 00:01:38.081
Python.
